{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's definitional gloss 'survived counterexample and remained flagged' smuggles in an unproven survival requirement: a flag that is sustained at adjudication need not have passed through any prior counterexample gauntlet. SRC_001 then baldly asserts the third conjunct — 'failure survived without rebuttal from the original artifact's proponents' — without citing any record of proponents being afforded a rebuttal opportunity or any evidence that no rebuttal was offered. A conjunctive criterion whose final conjunct is unevidenced is not demonstrated to be satisfied.","counterexample":["A critic flags an artifact at the initial verdict; no counterexample is presented at any stage; the artifact's proponents are never invited to respond; the adjudicator sustains the flag on the basis of the verdict's soundness alone. On SRC_001's own stated criterion the flag is NOT sustained (third conjunct fails), yet the ritual of §11.3 would sustain it — showing SRC_001's criterion diverges from the ritual's."],"premise":"The §11.3 ritual sustains a flag when the adjudication record shows the flag survives the adjudication stage; it does not additionally require that the underlying failure previously survived a counterexample and remained unrebutted by proponents. SRC_001 erects a three-part conjunction ('verdicts are sound' AND 'verdicts found failure' AND 'failure survived without rebuttal from the original artifact's proponents') and supplies no evidence for the third conjunct.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"000000000000","quote":"the ritual defines 'sustained' as 'survived counterexample and remained flagged'"},{"block":"000000000000","quote":"The sustained flag is the conjunction: (verdicts are sound) AND (verdicts found failure) AND (failure survived without rebuttal from the original artifact's proponents). All three are satisfied."}],"target":"f5c122ac8d682c37f67ea8eb04ac8b822caf386c4f34d49e49d4f7087708a648"}]}